[{:db/id 17592186287718 :question/problem "![](images/71/718ee39f4a0b2e9cca18ed4de77271a90139b13e8b71990611c1e13357245713.png)\n\nDescribe the shaded region shown above using set-builder notation.\n\n$$\n\\{(x,y)\\in {{field-1}}\\,:\\,y\\,{{field-2}}\\,-x+5,\\,y\\,{{field-3}}\\,x+3\\}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The points in our set include all those that lie *below* the line $y=-x+5$ and *below* the line $y=x+3.$\n\nA point $P(x,y)$ lies *inside* the shaded region if the following conditions are satisfied:\n\n$$\ny<-x+5,\\,y<x+3.\n$$\n\nSince the boundary is not included in the set (the lines are dashed), the inequalities are strict.\n\nMoreover, since we're considering *every* real value in this region, $x\\in R,y\\in R,$ and therefore \n\n$$\n(x,y)\\in R\\times R={R}^{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the set as\n\n$$\n\\{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,y<-x+5,\\,y<x+3\\}.\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186287722 :question/problem "![](images/67/67eeabfcff4eb24e14c15691c1c9de746a8870808197739577f7696faeada5bc.png)\n\nDescribe the shaded region shown above using set-builder notation.\n\n$$\n\\{(x,y)\\in {{field-1}}\\,:\\,y\\,{{field-2}}\\,4,\\,y\\,{{field-3}}\\,4x+1\\}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The points in our set include all those that lie *below* the line $y=4$ and *below* the line $y=4x+1.$\n\nA point $P(x,y)$ lies *inside* the shaded region if the following conditions are satisfied:\n\n$$\ny<4,\\,y<4x+1.\n$$\n\nWe must also include the lines $y=4$ and $y=4x+1$ since the boundaries are included in the set (the lines are solid). So, we change the corresponding inequality symbols above as follows:\n\n$$\ny\\le 4,\\,y\\le 4x+1\n$$\n\nMoreover, since we're considering *every* real value in this region, $x\\in R,y\\in R,$ and therefore \n\n$$\n(x,y)\\in R\\times R={R}^{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the set as\n\n$$\n\\{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,y\\le 4,\\,y\\le 4x+1\\}.\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186198477 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-ee4392fe-5c34-5631-8fcd-93d0be346505"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-ee4392fe-5c34-5631-8fcd-93d0be346505" :question/id #uuid "ee4392fe-5c34-5631-8fcd-93d0be346505" :question/math-academy-id "q-181693" :question/problem "![](images/3f/3f9807dae43519dbd9407130c51cb1473a36c46a42d8c433d9b4d88edb4e382d.png)\n\nUse set builder notation to describe the shaded region given in the diagram above:\n\n$$\n\\{(x,y)\\in {{field-1}}\\,:\\,x\\,{{field-2}}\\,2{y}^{2}\\}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The points in our set include all those that lie *to the right* of the curve $x=2{y}^{2}.$\n\nTherefore, a point $P(x,y)$ lies *inside* the shaded region if the following condition is satisfied:\n\n$$\nx>2{y}^{2}\n$$\n\nSince the boundary is not included in the set (the curve is dashed), the inequality is strict.\n\nMoreover, since we're considering *every* real value in this region, $x\\in R,y\\in R,$ and therefore \n\n$$\n(x,y)\\in R\\times R={R}^{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the set as\n\n$$\n\\{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,x>2{y}^{2}\\}.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-ebda1f69-fee2-52dd-85c4-c20bfd2e3b58" "answer-field-6778b0be-3214-5289-bc42-c4d9931ed8b3"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-ebda1f69-fee2-52dd-85c4-c20bfd2e3b58" :answer-field/id #uuid "ebda1f69-fee2-52dd-85c4-c20bfd2e3b58" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/select :answer-field/correct "answer-76d2c0ab-aee8-5245-b31a-1656f6a34d44" :answer-field/choices ["answer-64c0d4ca-3551-5246-9474-bf87dc44f240" "answer-a114218b-7eb8-53d8-acac-162934598611" "answer-76d2c0ab-aee8-5245-b31a-1656f6a34d44" "answer-41080398-b2ba-56b8-9658-937f33ed7f11"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-64c0d4ca-3551-5246-9474-bf87dc44f240" :answer/id #uuid "64c0d4ca-3551-5246-9474-bf87dc44f240" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "R" :db/ensure [:answer